No — and it's not on a release at all. This also corrects what I told you an hour ago.

The Mac mini reports `app_version 0.1.0`. That's the Xcode project's default `MARKETING_VERSION`, and the app sends `CFBundleShortVersionString`, which the plist defines as `$(MARKETING_VERSION)`. `mac-release.yml` overrides it per release tag, so a real release reports a real number — the installed `/Applications/Owletto.app` on this MacBook reports `12.1.0` (a genuine release, built 20 Jun). So the Mac mini is running a **locally built binary from Xcode**, not a downloaded DMG.

The part that matters for the #2361 verification is in `OwlettoUpdater.swift`:

```swift
// Local DEBUG builds are stamped MARKETING_VERSION=0.1.0 (CI overrides
// it per release tag), so a started updater would always see the
// published release as "newer" and nag "Update to vX.Y.Z" — a bogus
// prompt that also can't install cleanly over a DerivedData dev build.
// Don't start the scheduled updater in DEBUG.
#if DEBUG
let startingUpdater = false
```

So **waiting for the next release does not unblock the device checks**, which is what I said earlier and was wrong about. Cutting a release still produces the DMG automatically, but that Mac will never pick it up on its own. The Mac mini has to be rebuilt from current owletto main on that machine, or have a release DMG installed over the dev build.

On its vintage: it advertises `connector_manifests`, which landed 2026-07-05, so the binary is from on or after that date. I can't narrow it further from here — I'm on the MacBook Pro and the mini is a separate machine. Both Macs show the same `0.1.0` pattern, so neither is release-tracked.

Practical consequence: the two live checks for #2361 need someone at the Mac mini to rebuild Owletto from owletto main (which now carries #654, #656, #658, #659). Memory is corrected to say so, since the "just wait for a release" version of that note would have sent a future session down a dead end.

Worth knowing: a dev-built device worker is indistinguishable from a released one in `device_workers` except by that `0.1.0` tell — nothing marks a run as having come from an unreleased binary. If you ever want prod device runs to be traceable to a build, that's the gap.

